#include <turnkey/base>

/* The web half is the parent layer, apache-php, and fab-plan-resolve keeps a
   package the parent already carries at the parent's version, so nothing the
   parent installs is named again here: apache2, libapache2-mod-php, php-cli,
   libapache2-mod-security2, libapache2-mod-evasive, libapache2-mod-perl2,
   webmin-apache, webmin-phpini, adminer, libjs-jush and composer.

   The database server and webmin-postgresql are the plan of the
   unit.d/postgresql component, which fab resolves together with this one when
   the unit is present. They are not named here, and that is what makes the
   second artefact of this recipe an image with no server: remove the unit and
   the packages go with it, rather than being left behind by a name in this
   file. */

/* The four packages that make this stack PostgreSQL's rather than another
   engine's. LAMP names the same four roles with the MariaDB packages and
   nothing else differs, which is the point of decision 0013's layer model.

   Each is needed in both artefacts: the client and the drivers are how the
   stack reaches a database, and where that database lives is a setting, not
   a package. */
postgresql-client        /* the command line client */
php-pgsql                /* the PHP driver, which is what the stack runs on */
libdbd-pg-perl           /* the Perl DBI driver, for mod_perl2 from the parent */
python3-psycopg2         /* the Python driver */

/* project packages, resolved from the build host's staging repository: the
   declarative first boot that turns secrets.db_password into DB_PASS lives in
   inithooks, the console screens in confconsole, and keel is the instance
   description tool the appliance answers keel inspect with. core carries the
   stock inithooks and confconsole and no keel at all, so conf.d/main upgrades
   the two and checks the floor. */
inithooks
confconsole
keel

/* Deliberately not here, and each for its own reason, all four named in the
   changelog because dropping a package upstream ships is a content decision:

   python3-pygresql       upstream LAPP's second Python driver. One driver per
                          language per stack, and psycopg2 is the maintained
                          one; pygresql's own documentation points at it.
   libapache2-mod-python  upstream LAMP's, gone from Debian 13 and
                          unmaintained for Apache 2.4 long before that.
   php-xdebug             upstream LAMP's: a step debugger and profiler, which
                          belongs on a developer's machine and not switched on
                          in a published appliance.
   php-pear               upstream LAMP's, superseded by composer, which the
                          parent layer already carries.

   The first is this stack's and the other three are LAMP's. They are listed
   together on purpose: keeping any of them would make one of the two recipes
   differ from the other in something that is not the database, which is the
   one thing the composition is measured on. */
